meta { title = "Kalandra documentation", description = "Tutorials, language and built-in references, practical guides and contribution instructions.", order = 1, group = "Start here" }

Kalandra is a native language for CPU and GPU computing. Write typed functions, compose numerical operations and choose where tasks run. These pages describe the native development preview and its implemented language and tools.

h2 { "Learn Kalandra" }

Start with a guided introduction if you are new to the language. The tutorial follows the path from a first project to functions, collections and parallel work.

link { url = "/docs/tutorial", "Tutorial — learn the language by example" }

link { url = "/docs/installation", "Installation — choose and set up the toolchain" }

h2 { "Look something up" }

The language reference explains syntax, types and execution. The library reference lists every built-in call, including numeric conversions, collection operations, numerical functions and device selectors.

link { url = "/docs/language-reference", "Language reference" }

link { url = "/docs/library", "Built-in functions — alphabetical index" }

link { url = "/docs/tooling", "Compiler, checker, formatter and editor tools" }

h2 { "Solve a specific task" }

link { url = "/docs/parallelism", "Run independent CPU tasks" }

link { url = "/docs/gpu", "Transfer tensors and run a GPU function" }

link { url = "/docs/gpu-numerics", "Compose GPU numerical operations" }

link { url = "/docs/projects", "Configure a native project" }

link { url = "/docs/packages", "Install and update source dependencies" }

link { url = "/docs/troubleshooting", "Troubleshoot common problems" }

h2 { "A first program" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> int:
  6 * 7
""" }

Save this as main.k. Check the source, compile it, then run the executable; it displays 42.

codeblock { language = bash, """kalandra-check main.k
kalandra main.k -o program
./program
""" }

Unless a page gives a project layout, save each complete language example as main.k and use these commands. GPU examples use the Metal compilation commands in the GPU guide. Companion modules and intentionally rejected examples are labeled on their pages.

h2 { "Contribute" }

Learn how to build and test the compiler, locate the relevant implementation, improve documentation and submit a focused change. Compiler design documents and project planning remain in the repository.

link { url = "/docs/contributing", "Contributor guide" }

